BRUHAT-TITS DICTIONARY FOR THE n=16 DYADIC ROW
==============================================================================
  owner u=2^k        -> dyadic horosphere / tree depth k
  endpoint (16m+-1)/(16u) -> two unit boundary fibers over m
  protector p=2^j q  -> tree step with drop L=k-j
  odd q mod 16       -> boundary residue direction
  protected count    -> finite Hecke-kernel capacity

1. THM-367 AS A TREE-KERNEL MASS LAW
==============================================================================
For every checked owner depth k, active odd residue classes at a fixed
drop L have equal capacity, and their normalized total mass is 1.

  k owner drop active q mod 16          each capacity  active mass
  4    16    1 {1,15}                             1/2            1
  4    16    2 {1,3,13,15}                        1/4            1
  4    16    3 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  4    16    4 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1

  5    32    1 {1,15}                             1/2            1
  5    32    2 {1,3,13,15}                        1/4            1
  5    32    3 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  5    32    4 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  5    32    5 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1

  6    64    1 {1,15}                             1/2            1
  6    64    2 {1,3,13,15}                        1/4            1
  6    64    3 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  6    64    4 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  6    64    5 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  6    64    6 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1

  7   128    1 {1,15}                             1/2            1
  7   128    2 {1,3,13,15}                        1/4            1
  7   128    3 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  7   128    4 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  7   128    5 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  7   128    6 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  7   128    7 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1

  8   256    1 {1,15}                             1/2            1
  8   256    2 {1,3,13,15}                        1/4            1
  8   256    3 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  8   256    4 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  8   256    5 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  8   256    6 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  8   256    7 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1
  8   256    8 {1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}               1/8            1

Read: L=1 has two active boundary residues, each covering half the horosphere; L=2 has four active residues, each covering a quarter; L>=3 has eight active residues, each covering an eighth.

2. LOWER-PROTECTOR TRUNCATION
==============================================================================
The full residue kernel is symmetric in q mod 16.  A maximal owner,
however, can only use lower protectors p<u.  This takes a one-sided
truncation of the Bruhat-Tits apartment.

  owner u=16:
    drop  1: available={1}                      active lower={1}
    drop  2: available={1,3}                    active lower={1,3}
    drop  3: available={1,3,5,7}                active lower={1,3,5,7}
    drop  4: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}

  owner u=32:
    drop  1: available={1}                      active lower={1}
    drop  2: available={1,3}                    active lower={1,3}
    drop  3: available={1,3,5,7}                active lower={1,3,5,7}
    drop  4: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}
    drop  5: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}

  owner u=64:
    drop  1: available={1}                      active lower={1}
    drop  2: available={1,3}                    active lower={1,3}
    drop  3: available={1,3,5,7}                active lower={1,3,5,7}
    drop  4: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}
    drop  5: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}
    drop  6: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}

  owner u=128:
    drop  1: available={1}                      active lower={1}
    drop  2: available={1,3}                    active lower={1,3}
    drop  3: available={1,3,5,7}                active lower={1,3,5,7}
    drop  4: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}
    drop  5: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}
    drop  6: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}
    drop  7: available={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}     active lower={1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}

3. NINE-SPEED FAN AS A FINITE TREE STAR
==============================================================================
For u>=32 the constructive cover is:
  u/2 plus (u/32)*{1,3,5,7,9,11,13,15}.
It is one radial L=1 direction plus the full eight-direction boundary
sphere at L=5.  Its raw incidence mass is always 3u, so average
endpoint indegree is 3/2.

  owner gcd normalized cover                         fan indeg hist
     16   1 (8, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)           ((1, 30), (9, 2))
     32   1 (16, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)          ((1, 46), (2, 16), (9, 2))
     64   2 (16, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)          ((1, 92), (2, 32), (9, 4))
    128   4 (16, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)          ((1, 184), (2, 64), (9, 8))
    256   8 (16, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)          ((1, 368), (2, 128), (9, 16))
    512  16 (16, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15)          ((1, 736), (2, 256), (9, 32))

